<% content_for :title, "API docs" %>
<% notice_text = OpenTrust::LOOKUP_NOTICE %>
<div class="doc grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[200px_minmax(0,1fr)]">
  <aside class="hidden lg:block"><nav class="docnav sticky top-24 space-y-0.5" aria-label="On this page">
    <p class="px-2 pb-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">Guide</p>
    <a href="#libraries">Libraries</a><a href="#auth">Authentication</a><a href="#hashing">Send a hash</a><a href="#lookup">Check a number</a><a href="#batch">Check many</a><a href="#share">Share an experience</a><a href="#share-many">Share many</a><a href="#withdraw">Withdraw a report</a><a href="#reasons">Reason codes</a><a href="#me">Your checks</a><a href="#errors">Errors</a><a href="#responsibly">Using results</a>
  </nav></aside>

  <div class="min-w-0 max-w-3xl space-y-12">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">API docs</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg">A small JSON API. Base URL <span class="code">https://opentrust.in/api/v1</span>. Create your key from your <%= link_to "dashboard", portal_api_keys_path, class: "text-blue underline" %>.</p>
    </header>

    <section id="libraries">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Libraries</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">Small, dependency-free clients that wrap this API with typed errors, so you can be up and running in a few lines. They're open source<%= " and live in our " if OpenTrust::REPO_URL %><%= link_to "public repository", OpenTrust::REPO_URL, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer", class: "text-blue underline" if OpenTrust::REPO_URL %>.</p>
      <% unless OpenTrust::SDKS_PUBLISHED %>
        <p class="mt-3 rounded-md border border-amber bg-amber-wash p-3 text-sm">The Ruby and Node packages are about to be published. Until then you can call the API directly with any HTTP client, as shown below.</p>
      <% end %>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="codeblock"><div class="codeblock-bar">Ruby · gem</div><pre><code>gem install opentrust

client = OpenTrust::Client.new
client.lookup("9876543210").reported?</code></pre></div>
        <div class="codeblock"><div class="codeblock-bar">Node.js · npm</div><pre><code>npm install opentrust

const ot = new OpenTrust();
(await ot.lookup("9876543210")).reported</code></pre></div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm">Both read <span class="code">OPENTRUST_API_KEY</span> from the environment. See all <%= link_to "libraries and integrations", libraries_path, class: "text-blue underline" %>, including the Shopify and WooCommerce plugins on the way. Want a library for another language? <%= mail_to OpenTrust::FEEDBACK_EMAIL, "Tell us", class: "text-blue underline" %>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="auth">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Authentication</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">Send your key as a bearer token on every request. Keep it secret and revoke it from the dashboard if it leaks. Requests are limited to 120 per minute per key.</p>
      <div class="codeblock mt-4"><div class="codeblock-bar">header</div><pre><code>Authorization: Bearer ot_live_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx</code></pre></div>
    </section>

    <section id="hashing">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Send a hash instead of a number</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">You don't have to send us customers' phone numbers. Wherever an endpoint takes <span class="code">phone</span>, it also takes <span class="code">phone_sha256</span>: the SHA-256 of the number. We match on it without receiving the number, and a number and its hash find each other.</p>
      <p class="mt-3"><strong>The format has to be exact.</strong> A hash that was computed from a differently written number simply finds nothing, which looks like a clean customer. So:</p>
      <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>Keep the digits. Drop a leading <span class="code">91</span> (12 digits) or <span class="code">0</span> (11 digits). What is left must be 10 digits starting with 6, 7, 8 or 9.</li>
        <li>Put <span class="code">+91</span> in front: <span class="code">+919876543210</span>.</li>
        <li>SHA-256 of that text, as 64 <strong>lowercase</strong> hex characters.</li>
        <li>Send <span class="code">phone_length</span> with it: the length of the text you hashed. It is always <span class="code">13</span> (<span class="code">+91</span> and ten digits).</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-3"><strong>Why the length?</strong> It is a guard against the quiet mistake. A hash of <span class="code">9876543210</span> (10), <span class="code">09876543210</span> (11) or <span class="code">919876543210</span> (12) looks fine but never matches anything. If you send any other length we refuse the request with a message that says what probably went wrong, and it costs no check. Compute it from the text you actually hashed (<span class="code">len(number)</span>); copying a constant defeats the point.
      <div class="codeblock mt-4"><div class="codeblock-bar">test vector</div><pre><code>+919876543210  →  f3a47ce5ce3d4ca8ad15225a245b2759022f79489f5c62719b8c9490f7aab90e

# check it yourself
printf '%s' '+919876543210' | shasum -a 256</code></pre></div>
      <p class="mt-3">The libraries do all of this for you: <span class="code">OpenTrust::Client.new(hash_numbers: true)</span> in Ruby, <span class="code">new OpenTrust({ hashNumbers: true })</span> in Node. Both also export the hashing helper, and both are tested against the same <a href="https://github.com/parity/opentrust/blob/main/sdks/phone-hash-vectors.json" class="text-blue underline">test vectors</a> as this server.</p>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Check a number:</strong> <span class="code">{ "phone_sha256": "…", "phone_length": 13 }</span>. The result has <span class="code">phone_sha256</span> instead of <span class="code">phone_last4</span>, since we never saw the number.</li>
        <li><strong>Check many:</strong> <span class="code">{ "phone_sha256s": ["…", "…"], "phone_length": 13 }</span> instead of <span class="code">phones</span>, with one <span class="code">phone_length</span> for the whole list. Results come back in order.</li>
        <li><strong>Share an experience:</strong> <span class="code">phone_sha256</span> with <span class="code">phone_length</span> and <span class="code">phone_last4</span> (the last four digits, for display). We then hold the hash and the last four digits only. The same fields work in a batch.</li>
        <li><strong>Withdraw:</strong> <span class="code">phone_sha256</span> and <span class="code">phone_length</span>.</li>
        <li>Send <span class="code">phone</span> or <span class="code">phone_sha256</span>, never both.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3"><strong>Test your hashing.</strong> <span class="code">POST /phone_hash/check</span> with a <em>test</em> number and your hash returns what we compute and whether yours matches. It costs no checks. It takes the number, so never use it with a customer's.</p>
      <div class="codeblock mt-2"><div class="codeblock-bar">request</div><pre><code>curl https://opentrust.in/api/v1/phone_hash/check \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENTRUST_API_KEY" \
  -d phone="98765 43210" \
  -d phone_sha256=f3a47ce5ce3d4ca8ad15225a245b2759022f79489f5c62719b8c9490f7aab90e</code></pre></div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-soft"><strong>What a hash does and doesn't do.</strong> There are only about four billion Indian mobile numbers, so anyone willing to try them all can reverse a plain hash, including us. Sending hashes limits what is transmitted and stored (and what could leak from a log or a database); it does not make a number anonymous, and we treat a hash as personal data.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="lookup">
      <h2 class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold"><%= method_badge("POST") %> <span class="code !text-base">/lookups</span></h2>
      <p class="mt-2"><strong>Check one number.</strong> Uses 1 check from your allowance. Any common format works: <span class="code">98765 43210</span>, <span class="code">09876543210</span> or <span class="code">+919876543210</span>.</p>
      <div class="codeblock mt-4" data-controller="tabs">
        <div class="codeblock-bar" role="tablist"><button type="button" role="tab" class="codetab" data-tabs-target="tab" data-action="tabs#pick">curl</button><% if OpenTrust::SDKS_PUBLISHED %><button type="button" role="tab" class="codetab" data-tabs-target="tab" data-action="tabs#pick">Ruby gem</button><button type="button" role="tab" class="codetab" data-tabs-target="tab" data-action="tabs#pick">Node package</button><% end %><button type="button" role="tab" class="codetab" data-tabs-target="tab" data-action="tabs#pick">Node (fetch)</button><button type="button" role="tab" class="codetab" data-tabs-target="tab" data-action="tabs#pick">Python</button><button type="button" role="tab" class="codetab" data-tabs-target="tab" data-action="tabs#pick">PHP</button><button type="button" role="tab" class="codetab" data-tabs-target="tab" data-action="tabs#pick">Ruby (net/http)</button></div>
        <pre data-tabs-target="panel"><code>curl -X POST https://opentrust.in/api/v1/lookups \
  -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $OPENTRUST_KEY&quot; \
  -d phone=9876543210</code></pre><% if OpenTrust::SDKS_PUBLISHED %><pre data-tabs-target="panel"><code># gem install opentrust
require "opentrust"

client = OpenTrust::Client.new # reads OPENTRUST_API_KEY
result = client.lookup("9876543210")
result.reported?     # true / false
result.reports       # what other merchants noted
result.notice        # keep this in front of anyone who sees the result</code></pre><pre data-tabs-target="panel"><code>// npm install opentrust
